Sri Lanka has declared a state of emergency, appealing for international aid as Cyclone Ditwah's devastating rains and floods resulted in 132 confirmed deaths and 176 individuals still unaccounted for. The Disaster Management Centre (DMC), as reported by AFP, stated that the severe weather system obliterated over 15,000 homes and compelled 78,000 people to seek refuge in government-run temporary shelters. DMC Director-General Sampath Kotuwegoda confirmed, "We have 132 confirmed dead and another 176 missing" while noting that military forces, including the army, navy, and air force, have been deployed to bolster relief efforts.

As the cyclone tracked north toward India, the Sri Lankan government urged international aid and called on overseas Sri Lankans to donate funds for nearly half a million affected residents. India promptly responded, dispatching relief supplies and essential Humanitarian Assistance and Disaster Relief (HADR) equipment as part of their efforts. Prime Minister Narendra Modi, via a post on X, conveyed his condolences to Sri Lanka, mourning the cyclone's victims and reaffirming India's unwavering support for its "closest maritime neighbour". New landslides occurred in Kandy, 115 km (70 miles) east of Colombo, inundating several sections of the primary access road, while worsening floods in low-lying regions led authorities to mandate evacuations for residents along the Kelani River, which empties into the Indian Ocean from Colombo.
