Vietnam's 2025 Storm Season: Unprecedented Weather Patterns and Extensive Damage

As 2025 draws to a close, Deputy Minister Phung Duc Tien warned that the nation might still face its 16th storm of the year. The country has experienced highly atypical weather patterns and disasters, resulting in significant damage across the country, according to Hoang Duc Cuong, Deputy Director of the Hydrometeorological Department.

The year's natural disaster and hydro-meteorological developments have been marked by a host of uncommon and severe events. Storm No. 1 (WUTIP) emerged in the East Sea in June, an occurrence not witnessed in over four decades. The East Sea experienced an unprecedented surge in storm and tropical depression occurrences, marking the highest activity level recorded since Vietnam's documentation began in 1961.

Notably, the Bach Ma station in Thua Thien Hue recorded an extraordinary 1,739 mm of rainfall within a single day in October, a figure that the World Meteorological Organization (WMO) is in the process of assessing for potential recognition as one of the world's highest recorded 24-hour rainfall totals.

The floods of 2025 were extensive and intense, with river levels reaching unprecedented heights across numerous areas. These floods stretched from the Northern and Central territories to the Central Highlands, further impacting the lower Mekong Delta. Significantly, the historic flood in eastern Dak Lak, which was once a section of Phu Yen province, surpassed the 1993 flood peak levels on the Ba River basin.

In total, 21 storms and tropical depressions had been documented by the close of November, exceeding the former high of 20 established in 2017. Storm No. 9 (RAGASA) achieved level 17 wind speeds, with gusts surpassing this level, marking it as the most powerful storm ever documented in the East Sea.

The pattern of intensifying weather extremes is becoming more evident, with notable variations in storms, precipitation, and flooding directly impacted by climate change. Deputy Director Hoang Duc Cuong observed that the array of atypical occurrences in Vietnam and globally during 2025 indicates a shift in conventional flood patterns.
