A review of Google Street View images using AI across 16 states reveals a stark disparity in post-disaster recovery: structures in economically disadvantaged neighborhoods that sustained visible damage were prone to remain vacant plots for extended periods, whereas those in affluent regions were typically reconstructed into bigger and sturdier edifices.

Following a devastating natural catastrophe, certain individuals choose to remain and reconstruct their damaged homes. Entire communities undergo transformations that remain not entirely comprehended. Historically, scholars have tended to assess the effects of natural disasters by examining individual storms over limited durations, concentrating more on demographic shifts than on alterations to the built environment.

To overcome those constraints, Huang and his team utilized historical Google Street View data, comprising images captured of almost every address nationwide at intervals of one to three years. The researchers tasked GPT-4, the inaugural publicly accessible multi-modal model merging visual and linguistic capabilities, with examining Google Street View data to evaluate the recovery progress of buildings affected by extreme weather events across 16 states between 2011 and 2018.

Leveraging resources from a Stanford Institute for Human-Centered AI cloud credit, the team's research, now featured in Nature, reveals that structures left in disrepair in less affluent areas frequently stay unoccupied for extended periods, whereas more prosperous neighborhoods tend to restore and enhance them more swiftly. This inclination typically favors individuals who possess or govern financial assets.

The findings endorse the "recovery machine" theory, suggesting that, post-disaster, developers, realtors, and bankers are driven by their growth-oriented tendencies to reconstruct more optimally than before. This theory is supported by the team's observation that in regions with lower-income, over 37% of buildings that sustained damage and subsequently turned into vacant lots post-disaster persisted as empty lots for years. Conversely, this was the case for merely 22% and 7% of buildings in medium and high-income areas, respectively.

Further examination of FEMA aid recipients, organized by zip code, indicated that regions with a higher prevalence of homeowners and flood insurance experienced more significant rebuilding efforts. However, insurance costs have escalated to a level that is unaffordable for homeowners with lower incomes, according to the findings. He emphasizes that these insights highlight the critical need for implementing insurance subsidies.

Resilience and vulnerability are key concerns in the aftermath of natural disasters. Research has indicated that individuals with lower incomes or those in vulnerable positions often remain immobile following natural disasters due to insufficient resources for relocation. Known as the segmented withdrawal theory, this concept gained prominence following Hurricane Katrina when Black, impoverished residents of New Orleans appeared trapped in flood-prone, low-lying regions.

However, Huang and his colleagues' work with Google Street View supports the opposite theory that real estate developers and other forces that favor growth are more inclined to provide less support to lower-income neighborhoods, resulting in increased property abandonment among these residents. In Hwang's perspective, substantial community involvement is necessary to identify effective solutions for economically disadvantaged areas susceptible to climate disasters. "The present methods of aiding climate recovery in impoverished neighborhoods lack fairness," she remarks.
====ALIGNMENT====
[
