COLOMBO: Sri Lankan officials are contending with escalating floodwaters affecting sections of the capital following the havoc wrought by a formidable cyclone. According to an official, close to 200,000 individuals have been saved and relocated to shelters. Approximately 200,000 have been rescued and relocated to shelters, says official.

The number of fatalities has risen to 159 after a week of intense rainfall caused by Cyclone Ditwah. "Even though the cyclone has passed, significant rainfall upstream is causing flooding in the low-lying regions adjacent to the Kelani River," stated a DMC official. "Intense rainfall in the upstream regions is currently causing flooding in the low-lying areas adjacent to the Kelani River," stated a DMC official.

Numerous families remain confined in their homes due to the floodwaters in Colombo. Dozens of families remain stranded in their homes due to rising floodwaters in Colombo. The most severe flooding since the beginning of the 21st century took place in June 2003, resulting in the loss of 254 lives. Since 2017, Sri Lanka has faced its most lethal natural calamity with this cyclone, following the tragic flooding and landslides that resulted in over 200 fatalities and displaced hundreds of thousands.

Although the rainfall has diminished throughout the island, numerous roads in the most severely impacted central province continue to be impassable. Numerous routes in the hardest-hit central province continue to be impassable, according to the DMC. Over 20,000 homes have been devastated by the extreme weather system. Over 20,000 homes lie in ruins due to the severe weather system, displacing 122,000 individuals who now reside in government-operated temporary shelters.

An additional 833,000 individuals needed aid after being uprooted by the floods. An additional 833,000 individuals needed aid after being uprooted by the floods. Personnel from the army, navy, and air force have been dispatched. Military personnel from the army, navy, and air force have been dispatched to work in conjunction with civilian workers and volunteers, contributing to the relief efforts.

Access to the internet has faced interruptions as well. Authorities indicated that approximately one-third of the nation still lacks electricity and access to running water as a result of downed power lines and flooded water treatment plants. Authorities confirm power and communication services will resume in three days.

India promptly took the lead by dispatching relief supplies along with two helicopters and their respective crews for rescue operations. Japan announced plans to dispatch a team to evaluate urgent requirements and committed to providing additional support. Japan announced its plan to dispatch a team to evaluate urgent requirements and committed to providing additional support.
