global_errors.fused_text:
The Albanese government has announced the third round of funding from the $1 billion Disaster Ready Fund (DRF), a program dedicated to enhancing disaster resilience and mitigating risks. A total of $200 million has been allocated to fund 96 projects nationwide, aiming to enhance community resilience against extreme weather conditions.

From the total $200 million allocated by the federal government, a significant portion exceeding 50% is earmarked for infrastructure initiatives, with immediate construction initiation. Approved projects will enable various natural disaster relief initiatives, including establishing recovery centres, flood protection infrastructure, cyclone shelters, and sophisticated alert systems for natural threats.

Additional initiatives feature a $1.6 million infrastructure undertaking aimed at safeguarding Townsvale Estate in South Australia, a $14.1 million investment for enhancing stormwater infrastructure in Narrandera, located in Central NSW, and $895k to enhance the capacity of Karajarri Rangers, focusing on reducing severe wildfire threats in northwest WA.

The DRF has distributed approximately $400 million in federal funding to over 350 projects in the past two rounds. Almost 50% of these projects are currently in the phases of pre-construction or construction-delivery, with 43 projects either approaching completion or fully finished, as reported by the department.

The third round will advantage every one of the 538 local government areas spanning all states and territories, including Palm Island, a coastal area off Queensland known for its increased vulnerability to tropical cyclones and severe weather events, where a cyclone shelter and evacuation centre capable of accommodating 800 people will be constructed.

"Australians are well-acquainted with the annual onslaught of floods, bushfires, and cyclones, which is precisely why the Albanese government initiated the Disaster Ready Fund, allocating up to $1 billion over the span of five years," stated Kristy McBain, the emergency management minister.

The third round elevates the investment to make Australia more resilient through the DRF to $600 million, with the government and numerous project backers matching funding to support these initiatives.
