According to the Office of Civil Defence, the typhoon forced the displacement of over 560,000 villagers in the Philippines, with close to 450,000 of them being relocated to emergency shelters. Officials in charge of disaster response have issued a warning that a new tropical cyclone emerging from the Pacific has the potential to intensify into a super typhoon, threatening to hit the northern Philippines at the start of next week.

Kalmaegi unleashed a deluge equivalent to one-and-a-half months of precipitation in merely one day on Tuesday across metropolitan Cebu, according to state forecaster Benison Estareja. Heavy rains triggered flash floods, swelling a river and other waterways in Cebu city and its neighboring towns. Torrential floodwaters engulfed or carried off numerous vehicles in Cebu's residential areas, with dramatic footage captured by residents who were marooned on rooftops.

Six fatalities linked to Kalmaegi occurred when a Philippine military helicopter met a tragic end in the southern province of Agusan del Sur on Tuesday. Authorities also noted that an additional 62 individuals were unaccounted for in the central province of Negros Occidental, close to Cebu. Governor Pamela Baricuatro stated that the storm's impact might have been intensified by quarrying activities leading to river blockages and overflow, coupled with inadequate flood control measures in Cebu province.

A scandal surrounding inadequate or entirely absent flood control projects throughout the Philippines has ignited public indignation and led to street demonstrations in recent months. Furthermore, Cebu continues to recuperate from the 6.9 magnitude earthquake that struck on September 30, resulting in the collapse or extensive damage of numerous homes. The city is still reeling from the aftermath, with many residents struggling to come to terms with the devastating consequences of the storm and the earthquake.
