The Arab Center for Earthquake and Natural Disaster Prevention, established in 2004, recently orchestrated an advanced workshop in Algeria. The event, which took place on November 11-12, brought together specialists from ten Arab nations, including Syria, to evaluate earthquake hazards and instruct attendees on sophisticated systems.

The workshop, which partnered with the Global Earthquake Model (GEM) Foundation, provided training on the OpenQuake Engine software. Participants learned to craft detailed seismic hazard models and generate risk maps to evaluate potential earthquake effects. This training aimed to enhance regional collaboration in the fields of earthquake science and minimize risks associated with natural disasters.

Jamal Zakkour, who leads the National Earthquake Center, noted that the workshop facilitated the sharing of expertise and contemporary methods among the involved nations. This sharing of knowledge enhanced comprehension of seismic phenomena in the area and bolstered disaster response plans. Syria's involvement in the workshop is consistent with its initiatives to bolster scientific readiness as part of the Ministry of Emergency and Disaster Management's strategy.

The workshop offered significant insights into how Algeria assesses the risk of earthquakes. Participants gained valuable knowledge on developing accurate models to evaluate earthquake hazards and their possible impacts. The training encompassed using the OpenQuake Engine software to guide them in crafting accurate seismic hazard models and generating risk maps to evaluate earthquake impacts.
