COLOMBO, Dec. 9 (Xinhua) Humanitarian flood relief supplies from the Chinese government reached Colombo on Monday morning, where they were officially transferred to Sri Lankan authorities.

Chinese Ambassador to Sri Lanka Qi Zhenhong, Sri Lanka's Minister of Ports and Civil Aviation Anura Karunathilake, and Deputy Minister of Defense Aruna Jayasekera attended the handover ceremony.

Karunathilake expressed sincere gratitude for China's prompt and generous support, emphasizing that China has consistently offered support to Sri Lanka during each challenging period throughout the years. He expressed gratitude to the Chinese government and its citizens for their generous support.

Qi mentioned that in response to this serious natural disaster, all pertinent Chinese agencies promptly took action to offer assistance to their Sri Lankan counterparts. He observed that both Chinese enterprises and the Chinese diaspora in Sri Lanka have proactively engaged in aiding the local disaster relief initiatives. China remains committed to extending support to Sri Lanka to the best of its abilities, as it has consistently done in the past.

The Disaster Management Center of Sri Lanka reports that Cyclone Ditwah has unleashed devastating weather conditions across the nation, resulting in the tragic loss of over 600 lives and significant destruction impacting over 2 million residents. The cyclone has also demolished over 4,000 homes and inflicted partial damage on upwards of 60,000 residences across the country.

Among the supplies sent were tents, bed sheets, life jackets, and blankets, forming a component of the Chinese government's humanitarian aid to Sri Lanka. Karunathilake highlighted that the supplies delivered meet the critical needs of Sri Lanka, and he reiterated his aspiration for the lasting friendship between Sri Lanka and China. He noted that China's all-round support once again demonstrates that China is a trustworthy and reliable good friend and true friend of Sri Lanka.
