data.fused_content:
Government officials from both federal and Victorian levels are intensifying their support for families and primary producers impacted by January's bushfires in Victoria, building upon previous disaster recovery initiatives. Premier Jacinta Allan announced that uninsured homeowners who have lost their primary homes can qualify for payments reaching $52,250.

The Albanese government is working closely with Victoria in the recovery phase, with Emergency Management Minister Kristy McBain assuring Victorians of the government's unwavering support throughout both the fire response and subsequent recovery efforts. "We want the people of Victoria to be assured that the Albanese government supports you not only in responding to these fires but also throughout the recovery process," McBain stated.

Support programs focus on farmers and livestock access, with the Victorian government enhancing mental health and business assistance for farmers. The "Look Over the Farm Gate" initiative, managed by the National Centre for Farmer Health, is scheduled to commence on January 16. It will provide grants of up to $5,000 for local events and activities specifically in farming areas to support mental health and community recovery.

Counsellors who were active during earlier drought periods are set to either visit impacted farms or connect with farming families through phone calls. Steve Dimopoulos, the Acting Victorian Minister for Agriculture, stated that the strategy aims to address both safety and welfare concerns. In collaboration with Agriculture Victoria and the Fire Incident Controller, the efforts are focused on simplifying the process for farmers to monitor their animals' well-being, as they understand the distressing nature of this period.

The Victorian government is initiating the 2026 Victorian Bushfire Appeal to facilitate public donations. The government has assured that all contributions will be allocated to communities impacted by the fires, with the state covering administrative expenses. The appeal aims to distribute funds based on priorities set by local communities, in collaboration with charities and community organisations.

As access to affected regions improves, insurers have commenced claim processing and are dispatching personnel to these areas. From the Castlemaine Recovery Centre, ICA staff and several insurers are assisting policyholders with lodging claims and addressing queries, with plans for further outreach in additional communities as circumstances permit. Insurers have received 1,386 claims since January 7, spanning home, commercial, motor, and business interruption insurance.

Insurance companies have initiated protocols for bushfire and extreme weather conditions. IAG has established a mobile claims unit in Seymour to assist patrons of its affiliated brands, and has deployed mobile disaster hubs, along with customer support teams, assessors, and builders, into affected areas. Health funds are modifying their plans to accommodate customers in affected areas. HCF is providing qualified members in rural and remote locations with complimentary 15-minute online GP consultations via its collaboration with GP2U, ensuring appointments are accessible every day of the week.

Insurance providers are revising their policies for clients in disaster-stricken areas. HCF has rolled out a relief initiative for members affected by the Victorian bushfires and other severe weather occurrences. This initiative offers eligible members a premium waiver of up to three months, the ability to pause coverage for up to two years, and participation in the HealthyMinds Check-in program, which offers private consultations with certified psychologists, encompassing support for teenagers.
