Colombo: Sri Lanka has announced a state of emergency following heavy rains and floods that have increased the fatality count to 132, with an additional 176 individuals unaccounted for. The Disaster Management Centre (DMC) reports that over 15,000 homes have been devastated by the severe weather conditions.

President Anura Kumara Dissanayake has enacted emergency legislation, providing him with extensive authority to address the crisis. The military has successfully carried out the rescue of 69 bus passengers, employing both a helicopter and naval boats in the operation. The passengers were stranded in the Anuradhapura district following a 24-hour mission, and were assisted by navy sailors in reaching the roof of a neighboring house, employing ropes to navigate through the floodwaters safely.

The central district of Badulla saw its roads remain impassable, severing access to numerous villages and preventing the delivery of essential relief supplies. "Our village is isolated, with all access routes obstructed by landslides, preventing anyone from entering or exiting," said Saman Kumara from Maspanna village in Badulla. "We're facing a scarcity of food and have limited clean water supplies due to the collapse of power lines and the flooding of water purification facilities."

Cyclone Ditwah departed from the island on Saturday, charting a course northward towards India's shores. Chennai Airport in India has grounded 54 flights due to the weather department predicting exceptionally heavy rain and powerful winds. New landslides have struck Kandy's central district, situated 115 kilometres (71 miles) from Colombo, causing sections of the main access road to be submerged.

The government has called for international assistance and urged Sri Lankans living overseas to contribute financially to aid the impacted communities. According to officials, Prime Minister Harini Amarasuriya engaged with diplomats stationed in Colombo to brief them on the current circumstances and request support from their respective governments. India was the first to respond, dispatching two aircraft filled with essential relief supplies, while also donating its rations to help victims.

Indian Prime Minister Narendra Modi offered his condolences for the lives lost in Sri Lanka and affirmed that New Delhi stood prepared to dispatch additional assistance. Flooding in low-lying areas affects the residents settled along the banks of the Kelani River, which empties into the Indian Ocean from Colombo. While rainfall had subsided across much of the country, including the capital, persistent showers continued to affect the island's northern region, a lingering consequence of Cyclone Ditwah.

This cyclone marks the most lethal natural catastrophe in Sri Lanka since 2017, when floods and landslides claimed the lives of over 200 individuals and forced hundreds of thousands to seek refuge elsewhere. Approximately one-third of the nation is experiencing outages in electricity and water supply, while internet connectivity remains compromised in numerous regions.
