Emergency Shelters Established in Victoria and Saanich Due to Harsh Weather Conditions

In response to the region's harsh cold and powerful winds, emergency shelters have been established in Victoria and Saanich, triggering an Extreme Weather Response. The shelters are designed to provide relief to those without homes, with the District explaining that Extreme Weather Response beds are distinct from the standard nightly shelter accommodations.

In Saanich, the shelter situated at 3703 St. Aidan's St. is managed by Our Place Society, with financial backing from the District of Saanich provided by BC Housing. The spaces will be accessible as long as severe weather conditions fulfill provincial activation standards, which may encompass freezing temperatures, prolonged rain, or prolonged snowfall. The shelter accommodates individuals of all genders and has a strict no-pet policy.

Residents of Saanich have the option to utilize the shelter services provided by the Victoria Native Friendship Centre, which accommodates 50 individuals. Alongside the provisional sleeping arrangements at Broadview United Church, Saanich has additionally made daytime public warming facilities available. Locals are welcome to keep warm at municipal recreation centers and the Saanich municipal hall during their standard hours of operation.

The available facilities are Saanich Commonwealth Place, Gordon Head Recreation Centre, Cedar Hill Recreation and Arts Centre, and G.R. Pearkes Recreation Centre. According to the district, operational hours might fluctuate in response to severe weather, influenced by staffing availability.

In Victoria, the municipality reports the addition of 30 more shelter mats under the Emergency Weather Response initiative. Overnight accommodations are accessible at 525 Johnson St beginning at 8 p.m. on Friday and concluding at 7 a.m. on Saturday. The facility accommodates individuals of all genders and has a strict no-pet policy.

Forecasts indicate winds will increase to 30 kilometres per hour, with gusts possibly reaching 70 kilometres per hour near the Juan de Fuca Strait, before easing overnight. Overnight temperatures are expected to fall to approximately 1 C, with even chillier conditions anticipated further inland.
