COLOMBO - Sri Lanka declared a state of emergency on Saturday as the death toll from floods and landslides induced by Cyclone Ditwah climbed to 153, with 191 individuals unaccounted for. The Disaster Management Centre (DMC) reported that the severe weather conditions have devastated more than 20,000 homes, forcing 108,000 individuals into government-run temporary shelters, and 798,000 people needed aid after being temporarily displaced by the floods.

Personnel from the army, navy, and air force, along with civilian workers and volunteers, have been mobilized to support the relief operations. A passenger, recounting from the hospital, explained how navy sailors assisted them in climbing onto a nearby roof, using ropes to navigate them safely through the floodwaters. A helicopter had to cancel an initial rescue effort as the rotor downdraft risked dislodging the roof they were on. They were subsequently rescued by naval boats.

Clean water was scarce, and internet connections were also affected. Cyclone Ditwah departed the island on Saturday, heading towards neighboring India. In response to the cyclone's approach, Chennai Airport in India cancelled 54 flights, with meteorologists predicting extremely heavy rainfall and strong winds for the next 48 hours.

About a third of the country was without electricity or running water because power lines had collapsed and water purification facilities were inundated. In the central district of Badulla, roads remained impassable, isolating several villages and blocking relief supplies. A resident from one of the hardest-hit areas in Badulla conveyed that they lacked food and were running out of clean water.

The Sri Lankan government has called for international aid and encouraged Sri Lankans abroad to donate money to support affected communities. An Indian warship, on a pre-scheduled goodwill visit to Colombo, contributed its supplies to assist the victims.
