North Sumatra has been battered by severe weather conditions for several consecutive days, resulting in significant impacts on the region. In Tapanuli Selatan, the calamity of floods and landslides has claimed the lives of at least 38 individuals, with 58 individuals sustaining injuries, according to the national disaster agency BNPB.

The local disaster agency has dispatched heavy machinery to remove debris obstructing road access to the district, enabling rescue efforts to proceed. Three additional districts within the province have similarly faced the impacts of floods and landslides, with residents in Tapanuli Selatan evacuated from their homes, totaling 2,851 individuals.

As per the Meteorology, Climatology, and Geophysics Agency, extreme weather conditions are attributed to cyclone seeds, which are weather formations capable of developing into tropical cyclones. The yearly monsoon period, generally spanning from June to September, frequently results in intense rainfall, setting off landslides and flash floods.

Alterations in storm patterns due to climate change have affected both the length and strength of the season, resulting in increased rainfall, more frequent flash floods, and intensified wind gusts. This month, landslides caused by intense rainfall have claimed the lives of at least 38 individuals, with approximately 13 others still unaccounted for.
