The year's losses were predominantly due to severe weather, with the central tornado outbreak in March being the costliest event, amounting to $11 billion. This marked the largest March tornado outbreak ever documented. The Los Angeles wildfires emerged as the priciest incident of 2025, inflicting approximately $61.2 billion in destruction, effectively doubling the amount set by the previous record-holder. A billion-dollar drought, fueled by intense heat, struck the Western region in 2025, inflicting economic damage estimated at $3.1 billion.

Climate change is intensifying extreme weather events, stated Shel Winkley, a meteorologist from Climate Central. "Extreme weather is becoming more severe due to climate change, and the associated costs continue to rise," he added. The interval separating disasters has noticeably decreased, with the average narrowing to just 10 days in 2025, down from 82 days in the 1980s. This impacts all individuals — ranging from increased insurance premiums to public funds allocated for disaster aid and the upheaval of lives and livelihoods, Winkley stated.
