A lightning bolt stretching 515 miles has been recognized as the longest electrical bolt in the world, surpassing the previous record-holder by approximately 38 miles. The finding emerged from an analysis of a satellite image from the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration's GOES-16 series, which uncovered a lightning strike on October 22, 2017.

Until now, the longest lightning strike was thought to have occurred on April 29, 2020, and reached from an unspecified location, measuring 477 miles long. However, NOAA announced in an August 5 press release that the lightning bolt from 2017 had indeed set the new record.

The World Meteorological Organization set this new benchmark, noting that each of the lightning occurrences were identified as "megaflashes" due to their nature as "extremely long duration and distance lightning discharge events." The WMO reported that this unprecedented lightning bolt originated within a significant thunderstorm system.

According to the WMO announcement, the stretch from eastern Texas to near Kansas City mirrors the span from Paris to Venice in Europe. A car journey would last around eight to nine hours, while a commercial flight would require at least 90 minutes to traverse this distance.

The NOAA GOES-16 satellite, launched in 2016, captured images of both megaflashes in 2017 and 2020. As a component of the Geostationary Operational Environmental Satellite program, the satellite remains positioned over specific points on the Earth's surface.

Extreme weather events, including lightning, are becoming increasingly common due to climate change. The United States experiences approximately 25 million cloud-to-ground lightning strikes each year, resulting in 24,000 fatalities and 240,000 injuries. The National Centers for Environmental Information within NOAA monitors the financial impact of extreme weather events, which amounts to billions of dollars.

In 2024, they documented 27 significant natural disasters, comprising 17 severe storms, 5 tropical cyclones, and 2 winter storm/cold waves. According to NCEI's website, these 27 events collectively incurred costs totaling $182.7 billion. Anticipated rises in severe weather incidents, elevated temperatures, and various shifting climate patterns are likely to lead to more intense storms.

NOAA and NASA collaborate on behalf of the U.S. government to observe and analyze lightning, enhancing weather understanding and forecasting abilities. The financial burdens stemming from natural disasters in the U.S. are anticipated to increase by several billion dollars compared to 2024, according to NCEI's January report.
