In the past week, downpours have inundated southern and central Taiwan, leading to floods, landslides, and road closures, severely damaging properties and prompting the evacuation of thousands. The continuous rain forced the closure of schools and offices in certain regions throughout the week.

During last month's heated mass recall campaigns, several lawmakers from the Chinese Nationalist Party (KMT) exploited the timing of the typhoon to rally support against the recalls. The KMT recently shared an image of Tainan Mayor Huang Wei-che (黃偉哲) from the Democratic Progressive Party (DPP) attending a recall event in June, accusing him of prioritizing recall efforts over disaster relief. However, at the time the photo was captured, the typhoon had not yet developed.

The unsuccessful recall attempts on July 26 revealed the efficacy of the KMT's communication strategy, yet the use of biased narratives and misinformation undermine the public's ability to discern information critically.

As the KMT legislative caucus persisted in their unfounded critiques of the government's flood management initiatives, the storm's fierce winds and heavy rainfall led to agricultural losses exceeding NT$2.6 billion (US$86.6 million, while also wreaking havoc on over 23,000 roofs and toppling a record-breaking nearly 2,500 utility poles, resulting in widespread power failures.

During the intense mass recall campaigns last month, a typhoon struck, prompting visits to the affected areas by the president, premier, and local government officials. Chiayi Mayor Huang Min-hui (黃敏惠) from the KMT expressed gratitude towards Kaohsiung Mayor Chen Chi-mai (陳其邁) of the DPP for quickly dispatching personnel to assist Chiayi City with relief efforts. Meanwhile, KMT Legislator Niu Hsu-ting (牛煦庭) criticized the sharing of photos depicting Taiwan Power Co workers repairing utility poles post-typhoon, labeling it as "emotional blackmail" of voters, especially since KMT lawmakers had previously reduced a subsidy for the utility.

The Central Weather Administration (CWA) shared a chart on Facebook illustrating the intensity of the brief yet intense rainfall that affected southern Taiwan. The chart connected hourly rainfall statistics with "common perceptions" to aid public comprehension of various rainfall intensities. For instance, it equated less than 0.1mm of rain per hour to “the refreshing mist from water sprayers at summer amusement parks.

Several weather stations reported rainfall surpassing 100mm per hour, notably recording 122mm per hour in Pingtung County’s Sandimen Township. According to the CWA, rainfall amounts greater than 80mm per hour are described as “creating a feeling of pressure or asphyxiation akin to the sky falling; overwhelming the drainage capabilities of most urban areas; and likely to result in flooding and landslides.

The CWA characterized rainfall exceeding 80mm per hour as “invoking a feeling of pressure or suffocation akin to the sky collapsing; surpassing the drainage system capacity of most cities; and readily leading to floods and landslides. This intensity is confined to severe weather events, and for many, it might be a once-in-a-lifetime occurrence, they noted.

It is essential for the government to address this issue earnestly and devise clear and swift methods to communicate facts and policies effectively, aiming to reduce the dissemination of damaging misinformation, not only from opposition groups but also from China. This communication must be woven throughout government channels to restore public confidence and fortify resilience.
