China's Flooding Disaster Claims Dozens of Lives

Severe rainfall and subsequent flooding have resulted in the deaths of at least 60 individuals throughout northern China, including the capital city of Beijing, since late July. The disaster has been attributed to extreme weather patterns associated with climate change.

According to state media, flash floods in China's Gansu province have resulted in at least 10 fatalities, with 33 individuals reported missing. The floods were triggered by relentless downpours that started on August 7. Chinese President Xi Jinping has commanded rescue efforts to be carried out with complete dedication and maximum exertion to locate the missing individuals.

Faced with the "frequent occurrence of extreme weather," Xi instructed all regions to "resolutely overcome complacency" and strengthen efforts to pinpoint risks. In recent weeks, China's northern and southern regions have been battered by record-breaking rainfall, prompting flood warnings across 11 provinces.

Images released by the authorities in Gansu depicted roads blanketed with silt and sizable stones, while a video released by Chinese fire officials on Weibo showed rescuers leading people through rushing murky grey water. The floods have also resulted in the deaths of at least 30 individuals in Beijing, with 31 individuals caught in an elderly care facility among the victims.
