On Boxing Day, Alberta's two major urban centers, Calgary and Edmonton, were placed under blowing snow advisories, with extensive regions throughout the province experiencing a range of winter weather advisories or warnings. Environment Canada stated that blowing snow advisories have been issued due to reduced visibility caused by the interaction of wind and precipitation. "Expect travel to be perilous with nearly nonexistent visibility," stated the weather agency. "Conditions are anticipated to progressively get better by Saturday afternoon."

Throughout the day, brisk winds combined with dropping temperatures will lead to wind chill values approaching -35. Drivers have been urged by Environment Canada to steer clear of unnecessary travel in regions affected by a blowing snow advisory until road conditions become safer.

Other municipalities in the province were likewise issued blowing snow advisories on Friday afternoon. Amid cold weather alerts, residents in certain regions were cautioned to prepare for possible blizzard-like conditions on Saturday.

Frigid conditions persist in Edmonton. The city announced in a news release that its extreme weather response will conclude on Sunday afternoon, anticipating a temperature improvement in the coming week. Environment Canada forecasts that Edmonton's temperature will drop significantly, reaching a low of -26 C on Saturday, with a projected high for Monday of 2 C. In Edmonton, the city issued a Friday update concerning its extreme weather measures, which have been operational since December 19 due to frigid conditions.
