TUOLUMNE COUNTY, Calif. (AP) — A rapidly advancing wildfire consumed homes in a California Gold Rush town, originally established around 1850 by Chinese miners who had been expelled from a neighboring camp. Chinese Camp originated when Chinese miners established themselves there after being expelled from a neighboring camp, as stated by Visit Tuolumne County. During the Gold Rush, a multitude of Chinese immigrants arrived in California but were met with widespread discrimination and hostility, including the imposition of a steep Foreign Miners Tax intended to force them out of the mining industry.

The blaze swiftly grew to encompass 10 square miles, prompting the evacuation of the town of Chinese Camp and nearby highways on Tuesday, as reported by CalFire, the state's primary firefighting agency. There were no initial reports indicated any injuries or fatalities. Among several blazes known as the TCU September Lightning Complex, this fire has consumed over 19 square miles across various areas.

At least five residences were ablaze in the rural area, characterized by a combination of standalone and mobile homes. On Tuesday evening, outside a residence, efforts were made in a desperate attempt to keep the blaze from spreading from the neighboring house. An RV on the property was damaged by the fire. Their efforts lasted approximately 30 minutes until the firefighters arrived.

Fire authorities have called for more resources, including fire engines, bulldozers, and an aircraft. According to CalFire, this fire is among over a dozen that ignited across California on Tuesday, ignited by lightning. Fire authorities have also determined that the 6-5 Fire originated from a lightning strike. By Wednesday morning, containment efforts had not been successful.
