Climate Change Devastates UK Wheat Harvests, Threatening Bread Supplies

A report by the Energy and Climate Intelligence Unit (ECIU) has revealed that the UK has lost an entire year's supply of bread due to extreme weather conditions caused by climate change. Since 2020, the country has experienced two of the worst three harvests on record, with the worst year being 2020 and the third worst 2024.

The intensification of droughts and floods has resulted in a shortfall of more than 7 million tonnes in wheat production, equivalent to over 4 billion loaves of bread. This scarcity is straining international supplies, as other nations face their own challenges due to conflicts and severe weather conditions.

Farmers are struggling to cope with the unpredictable weather patterns, which are rendering their crops less productive. Colin Chappell, a crop farmer from Lincolnshire, remarked, "Farming has never been more difficult. While I'm accustomed to handling the harshest weather conditions, the recent years have turned unmanageable. Extended periods of rain are succeeded by stretches of drought, appearing relentless, and I am now confronting a second consecutive disastrous harvest."

The situation is anticipated to deteriorate further as climate change leads to extended dry spells combined with more intense and severe rainfall, potentially resulting in floods. According to the ECIU, this decade has seen two of the three poorest harvests ever recorded, with expectations that conditions will worsen.

The climate emergency is generating unpredictability for farmers, as various growers encounter alternating periods of prosperity and hardship. Tom Lancaster, the analyst specializing in land, food, and farming for the ECIU, commented, "This exemplifies the reality of farming amidst climate change. Unpredictable weather patterns are rendering our bread less British, compelling millers to rely on imports because of deficiencies in UK yields."

The UK's wheat self-sufficiency decreased from 96% in 2023 to 79% in 2024, highlighting the need for support to aid farmers in adjusting to these harsh conditions. Colin Chappell emphasized, "but with the green farming schemes here in England closed, that support isn’t open to me. Getting them open has to be a priority if farmers are to be able to continue to grow wheat and other key crops for the Great British public."
