The Environment Agency has finalized 151 flood management projects throughout England from July 2024 to 2025. The Government has declared that these initiatives have significantly enhanced flood protection for over 24,000 homes and businesses nationwide. According to ministers, the agency implemented these flood schemes, which included the construction of new defenses and upgrades to existing ones, within the year following Labour's rise to power last July. This progress signifies that over 24,000 properties now enjoy increased protection, as the agency aims to safeguard 52,000 properties by April 2026.

The completed flood schemes encompass a range of projects, including the Saltfleet to Gibraltar Point Beach Management scheme in Lincolnshire, which aims to better protect 2,904 local properties, and the Cockett Wick seawall improvement scheme in Jaywick, which is better protecting 1,526 properties. Additionally, work finished on the £75 million Canvey Island Flood Defence Scheme in July, which renewed three kilometres of tidal defences in Essex to better protect more than 6,000 properties for the next 50 years.

The Government has pledged to boost flood defence spending, committing £2.65 billion over the next two years until April 2026, marking a £250 million increase over the previous Tory government's allocation. More widely, the Government has promised £7.9 billion in flood defences from 2026 to 2036, bringing the total to more than £10.5 billion over the 12 years from 2024. This investment is set to benefit more than 890,000 properties, protecting businesses, safeguarding farmland and allowing the Government to push ahead with its plans to build 1.5 million new homes by 2029.

The Environment Agency says 6.3 million properties in England are currently at risk from flooding, and estimates that this number will rise to around eight million (one in four properties) by 2050. Flooding has a devastating impact on communities across the country and will continue to worsen as extreme weather is caused by our changing climate. The agency will continue to work with the government to deliver its long-term flood investment programme, ensuring the most vulnerable communities get the protection they need.
